{"root":"pli/ms","translators":["sujato"],"title":"The Increase of Wisdom","html":"<article id='sn55.61'><header><ul><li class='division'><span class='root'>Saṁyutta Nikāya 55.61 </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Linked Discourses 55.61 </span></li>\n<li><span class='root'>6. Sappaññavagga </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>6. A Wise Person </span></li></ul>\n<h1 class='sutta-title'><span class='root'>Paññāvepullasutta </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>The Increase of Wisdom </span></h1></header>\n<p><span class='root'>“…. Paññāvepullāya … saṁvattantī”ti. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>“Mendicants, when four things are developed and cultivated they lead to the increase of wisdom. …” </span></p>\n<p class='endsutta'><span class='root'>Ekādasamaṁ. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></p>\n<p class='endvagga'><span class='root'>Sappaññavaggo chaṭṭho. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></p>\n<p class='uddana-intro'><span class='root'>Tassuddānaṁ </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></p>\n<blockquote class='uddanagatha'><p><span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Sagāthakaṁ vassaṁvutthaṁ, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>dhammadinnañca gilānaṁ; </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Caturo phalā paṭilābho, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>vuddhi vepullatāya cāti. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></span></p></blockquote></article>"}
